>
> It may be my relative newness to the game (I haven't ascended, though
> I've got a valk on the VS), but I've found that autopickup leads to
> grabbing things I don't care about.  I'm not a very tidy player though,
> and tend to leave things where they fall.
>
> What would be sane set of autopickup rules?  gold?  Nope.

In the early game, you want gold. Later on, just kick it out of pickup_types
in the options menu.

>                                                            Scrolls?
> Maybe, they're useful.  Food, stones, weapons, armor?  

Setting autopickup exceptions to only pickup useful food (in terms
of nutrition/weight) is easy enough (also, refine those later in the
game). Ditto for weapons - handy for your own thrown missiles (make a
rule to match on a name you give your missiles with #name-y). If you're
paranoid about missing random generated super armor, include magical
armor names in the exceptions.

>                                                       Nope.  Books?  I
> love reading, but I don't need to pick them up every time.

You'll want to pick them up on first encounter. After that, they go to
the stash.

> I find it's much cleaner to turn it off, then explicitly pick up when I
> want something.

Sometimes I'll turn it off around rodney-time or a little earlier,
because I really won't want anything anymore, but especially in the
early game, a big part of which centers around collecting stuff, a nice
set of autopickup options helps a lot, I think.

